Fail, not die, when the .lzma dictionary does not fit in memory.
The dictionary size comes from the header, up to 4 GiB, and was
allocated with xmalloc(). When that failed, unlzma died, leaving an
empty output file and skipping the remaining operands. On a 32-bit
system any file with a large dictionary did this. Use malloc() and
return -1 with "out of memory" so that the caller can clean up.

---
 archival/libarchive/decompress_unlzma.c |    7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/archival/libarchive/decompress_unlzma.c
+++ b/archival/libarchive/decompress_unlzma.c
@@ -261,7 +261,12 @@
 		header.dict_size++;
 
 	buffer_size = MIN(header.dst_size, header.dict_size);
-	buffer = xmalloc(buffer_size);
+	/* The header may ask for up to 4 GiB: fail instead of dying */
+	buffer = malloc(buffer_size);
+	if (!buffer && buffer_size != 0) {
+		bb_simple_error_msg(bb_msg_memory_exhausted);
+		return -1;
+	}
 
 	{
 		int num_probs;
